                <translation language="English"><![CDATA[<cover src="WECoverFront.hsm"><font size=12 style=b face=courier color=000000><margin right=56 left=56 top=40 bottom=40>

<PB><p align=center>A Journey Without An Ending

<font style=n>By Max


Part 3: The Ending Has Not Yet Been Written

October 30, 2012

<PB><PB><font style=i size=10><p align=left>Disclaimer: This series of articles is written mainly based on known facts or research. Although Max has taken some liberties or "artistic license" in some parts, like in adapting or intertwining the gameplay or the fiction into facts of reality, the article has been fact-checked by others in the GoMe and in most aspects is, to the best of our knowledge, factually accurate.

<PB><PB><font style=n size=10><p align=left>We learned in the first part of this series how John Loftin and Elias Zandi made their first expedition to D'ni in 1988 utilizing lava tunnels located in the desert in New Mexico. During later expeditions, they managed to explore the cavern and get their first glimpses of the D'ni culture and history.

In the second part, we learned how the D'ni Restoration Council (DRC) was eventually founded after Elias Zandi passed away, leaving the property to his son Jeff Zandi, while he left his vast fortune to the members of the DRC to continue the restoration. By the end of 1997, the DRC had established themselves in the cavern on permanent basis, overseeing the restoration and further exploring the D'ni and the different Ages.

We also learned how the game company Cyan (Worlds), Inc. in Spokane, Wash. had been inspired by the findings and the history of D'ni to create a story based puzzle game called Myst (see Appendix 1).

In this last concluding part, we take a look at the DRC's activities in the cavern leading up to the final exodus, as well as a look to the future of D'ni as it stands now.

The DRC had a very active period from 1997 to 2004. They continued to meticulously explore the cavern and the different Ages they had discovered. The group at that time consisted of:

* Dr. Richard Watson, still council chairman and the one overseeing the entire restoration.
* Marie Sutherland, born in Paragould, Arkansas, who had a doctorate as an anthropologist and had been previously down on an expedition along with Dr. Watson before joining DRC.
* Dr. Ikuro Kodama, born in Long Beach, Ca., with a BA as a mechanical engineer and a Tech Doctorate in Geology and who was a friend of Dr. Watson and had already in 1994 visited the Cavern for the first time.
* Viktor Laxman, who was born in UK and educated as an engineer at Cambridge and in 1995 learned about the project from Dr. Watson.
* Michael Engberg, who was born in Sacramento, Ca. was a Master of Building Science and had visited the Cavern in 1998 before joining the DRC group in 1999.

That group along with several contractors (see Appendix 2) kept exploring all the Ages they could link to. When an Age was tested and finally regarded as stable and safe, then the Linking Book leading there would always bear the DRC stamp. A few of the contractors were made responsible for making studies and doing research in the different Ages, like Douglas Sharper did in Teledahn. His journal can still be found in his city office on Ae'gura for us to read.

<PB><font style=b size=9><p align=center>Opening The Cavern

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>From 1997 to 2004, the DRC lay the groundwork for a systematic step-by-step restoration of the cavern and categorized the restoration work into five different phases, as listed on the DRC website (see in Links below). More equipment and tools were brought down during 1999 and the restoration of the main island of Ae'gura was started in 2000.  It is one of the largest public areas explorers have access to at the moment, although some areas of the city are still closed off for us explorers and will remain so for safety reasons.

In November 2001, Viktor Laxman came across a small handheld piece of technology that later was named the KI (pronounced "key"), which was due to the fact that KI, the D'ni numeral for "3", was engraved on the device. Laxman performed extensive testing and research on the first generation and several other versions over the years.  He managed to convert the interface of the KI to use characters and numerals from the surface instead of D'ni. DRC members began to use the KI fully in 2002.

The KI was later made available to explorers and for years has been the standard way to communicate across the cavern and the various Ages instantly. It should be noted that the reliability of the KI device has always been questioned, as it is dependent on an ancient technology that Laxman never quite managed to make completely stable or was not capable of fully understanding all of its functions.

During 2001, much of the DRC's effort went into the restoration of several neighborhoods (hereafter called "hoods"). These hoods were small, self-contained community areas located in what is known as the "City Proper", the largest part of the D'ni empire built within and along a large portion of the cavern wall. A few of them were well-preserved and could be easily restored, so in March 2002, the DRC opened several for public access by adding links to the Nexus.

Finally in 2002, the DRC allowed the first authorized visitors down to the cavern, also allowing access to a number of hoods and a few of the different Ages that had been checked and deemed safe to visit.  In September of that year, Phil Henderson, one of the contractors and a restoration engineer, went missing while surveying a tunnel in Eder Kemo. Dr. Watson received a message on his KI a few weeks later from Henderson, stating he was ok, but would not come back. Despite that, he would later return after a year of absence, but those who knew him felt he was more estranged and distant.  He began making confusing statements and writing obscure notes as seen in his journal on his Relto. His return was perhaps not a good omen.

<font style=b size=9><p align=center>Differing Views

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>In the later half of 2003, explorers were actually "invited" via e-mail by Jeff Zandi to come and visit D'ni. This was Zandi's way stating that the cavern was open to the general public and the DRC had let go of some of the restrictions. This was not necessarily true, and at the end of 2003, it was clear that procedures on approving Ages and areas of the cavern were causing some severe frictions within the DRC. One notable example occured when Douglas Sharper made a stand against his employers and helped explorers tear down the barriers barring access to the Kahlo Pub in Ae'gura.

At the end of 2003, there was yet another casualty within the DRC ranks.  Phil Henderson was believed to have been killed when an unstable wall collapsed down on him during an expedition in the cavern. Strangely enough, no body was found, only his KI. At the same time, even as security was increased after Phil's apparent death, visitors continued to come down and disregarded the warnings of taking extreme caution, much to DRC's, particularly Dr. Watson's, dismay. To quote him from his journal: "Despite our warnings, despite our statements that we're moving and working as quickly as we safely can, it wasn't fast enough for them. They just don't comprehend. We've been working down here for over ten years! Yet they think they know better, that we can 'just approve areas more quickly.' I have tried to impress upon them the importance of moving slowly. Deliberately. Carefully. My warnings seemed to fall on deaf ears. Their enthusiasm is understandable. Their disregard for the safety of the people down here is not."

With a new year of 2004 beginning and that fatal accident in mind, Dr. Watson started to have serious doubts about the restoration, or even if it should continue at all.

<font style=b size=9><p align=center>On Hiatus

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>In the end, in February 2004, mostly due to financial difficulties and the disappearance of Dr. Richard Watson to the surface, the DRC decided to leave the cavern.  As such, the huge restoration project spanning over two decades was put on indefinite hold. One by one, members of the DRC took their leave, and Douglas Sharper recorded the events in his journal before leaving too. Despite this, explorers continued to explore, even though the DRC clearly stated that they were on their own for now.

"03.22.2004 - Is someone else here? I'm not sure I'm alone anymore. I'm going to have to be careful." and later "03.23.2004 - There is someone here. I'm fairly certain of it now. But perhaps this isn't such a bad thing." Once again, the Cavern seemed to sink into silence, the noise of countless restoration workers gone.

<font style=b size=9><p align=center>Freedom At Last

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>Still, all was not quiet in the cavern. In 2005, Yeesha returned to the cavern to find a way to complete the mission she had previously failed to do: free the Bahro from their enslavement once and for all and thus lift the burden on her and her people. To do this, she called upon none other than Dr. Richard Watson, who had somehow come back down to the cavern and met up with her, to unlock and use an ancient tablet that was key to controlling the Bahro.

During his journey to release the tablet, Dr. Watson also encountered a D'ni man named Esher, an expert on the Bahro who had retreated to their home Age of Noloben after the Fall of D'ni.  Through gruesome experimentation, he learned the weaknesses of the Bahro and even gained their linking abilities via a patch of skin taken from one of them.

Eventually, Watson was able to unlock the tablet and free the Bahro by releasing the tablet that controlled them into their custody.  Yeesha was released from her burden and, according to accounts of the event, she took Watson to the D'ni's new home of Releeshahn where Esher would answer to the Bahro for his crimes. She was also happily reunited with her father Atrus, who was still alive due to his D'ni heritage.

While the Bahro's newly-acquired freedom was a joyous occasion, it would not be without consequences.

<font style=b size=9><p align=center>A New Lease

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>Despite a year of little to no contact, the DRC was still active. Cate Alexander had joined the group in 2005 and through her influence in the business world, soon managed to get Turner Broadcasting to fund continued restoration of D'ni. So in January 2007, the DRC returned to the cavern, once again with substantial financial backing.

During this period, there were several major events, especially from May 2007 and forward. All of it is of such an extent that it would be too much to include it all in this series of articles. Instead, further reading from the various links below this article is recommend, as well as visiting during Heritage Nights, where those events are retold by DimensionTravelerCalum and others.

Over that year, the DRC granted access to several new Ages, like Minkata, the Garden Ages of Eder Delin and Eder Tsogal, the as-yet-unnamed pod Age and its various observation pods. New areas of the cavern were also opened, such as the various Guild Pubs corresponding to the five Guilds promoted in Kirel. More of the mansion on K'veer was also opened.

<font style=b size=9><p align=center>Tragedy and Possible War

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>Unfortunately, all was not good news during this period. A very tragic event in May 2007 took the lives of Willow "Wheely" Engberg, the daughter of DRC member Michael Engberg, and Rosetta Taylor, her friend. While they explored near the Kahlo Pub, they found a crack that had appeared due to several earthquakes that had happened recently.  When they entered the crack to investigate, another earthquake caused more debris to fall, killing Rosette and trapping Wheely.  DRC restoration engineers worked around the clock to free her, but did not reach her in time.  The sight of where she had been trapped was described as very gruesome.

Before her death, Wheely had mentioned the presence of Bahro numerous times via her KI.  As such, many blamed the Bahro for this crime.  As the year went on, there were more instances of Bahro activity, both in the cavern and in the Ages, with some even appearing on explorers' Relto Ages. At one point, Douglas Sharper and a team traveled to Noloben to investigate further, ending with Sharper killing a Bahro in alleged self-defense before returning (some theorized he was actually avenging Wheely's death).  All this, along with the unprecedented returns of Phil Henderson and Richard Watson had many explorers wondering what would happen next.

<font style=b size=9><p align=center>Another Ending?

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>Tensions seemed to reach their peak near the end of that year and Bahro began to swarm the cavern, concentrated around Kerath's Arch.  Yeesha returned from Releeshahn to K'veer and warned explorers of a possible Bahro civil war over the humans' presence in the cavern and Ages, with the faction opposed to it being led by the Bahro that had been mutilated years before by Esher.  She would use whatever she could at her disposal to lure them away from the cavern and then promptly linked away to do so.  The hostile Bahro soon followed, and the cavern settled into relative tranquility once more.  Neither has been heard from since, leading many to believe that Yeesha was successful.

Despite renewed excitement over the restoration of D'ni and enthusiastic explorers, budget realities soon prevailed. Turner decided to withdraw from their obligation and end their financial support of the restoration work at the beginning of 2008. The cavern would soon be shut down once more.

On the announced last day of April 9th, D'ni was host to a massive farewell celebration. Saddened explorers took one last look at their favorite Ages or places in the cavern. There were many tear-stained eyes as some said their goodbyes to good friends and fellow explorers. Even some of the Bahro linked into the Watcher's Pub to pay their respects. People listened to DJ's playing music, danced and enjoyed themselves. Many vowed that despite this new setback, the ending had truly not been written, and groups outside the DRC did much to keep the spirit alive.

Indeed, many explorers never actually left after that. I made a point to discuss this with Jeff Zandi during our talk after I learned about it. He never really gave a definite answer, but simply said "Well, if there were in fact a few who chose to stay, it sure showed some dedication...and genuine love for D'ni!" and added with a wry, almost cheeky smile "And if so, what harm could they do down there?" I myself am totally convinced he knew much more than he was willing to admit.

<font style=b size=9><p align=center>Third Time Is The Charm

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>A couple of years passed. Then one day, 4th of February 2010, Viktor Laxman made an entry on the DRC forums. Something, or someone, had triggered security sensors down in the cavern. A few days later, an expedition consisting of former DRC members made a trip down and came upon a group of explorers. Some of them had indeed stayed down there for a while; others had discovered the cavern just recently, much to the group's astonishment. It later turned out that Dr. Watson had been down there too, and it had been him triggering the sensors. The DRC was delighted to find activity in the cavern again. Everyone was told they could stay, but there would be no assistance or restoration efforts from the DRC or any ResEng until proper funding could be secured.

And yet again, D'ni had risen up from the ashes. During that month, the word started to spread, and soon, the streets of Ae'gura started to come to life again with thousands upon thousands of pairs of running and keenly exploring feet.

And so it has been, from then until today.

Even now, there are still many questions which have yet to be definitively answered:

* What IF... we, in fact, have some remote connection (going back more than thousands of years) to the D'ni?
* What IF... some archaic memories we might have originate from this?
* What IF... that explains the "call" many of us (but not everybody) have felt that has drawn us to this place?
* What IF... Elias Zandi was actually unknowingly right: that some of us might indeed have some D'ni blood?


<font style=b size=9><p align=center>Ending Thoughts

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>To round off this series, some final words. One can safely say that the cavern is inhabited again, and there are still plenty of new explorers that find their way to D'ni on a daily basis and take the journeys like so many of us have done before them. There are regular events held in the different locations and Hoods. Like D'ni Language classes, Heritage Nights, monthly All Guilds Meet at Kirel etc.

In the DRC's absence, some privately managed and financed enterprises have stepped in and taken over the further development. They are now working hard to restore more Ages and parts of the cavern to eventually make them available to regular explorers. Some have even found the means to Write their own Ages.  This is a slow and time-consuming task, and has to be, as safety is still a concern. So far, only a few new Ages have emerged. But the prospects are promising, and explorers have been known to have to bide their time in the past, like they are doing now.

But we can't forget the ones that made this all possible. Had John Loftin and Elias Zandi been able to see all the amazement and awe in the faces of new visitors to D'ni, I'm sure both would have a very content smile on their faces. Likewise, Jeff Zandi, who has taken the task to give newcomers a bit of guidance, always welcoming them with a friendly smile.

And we must also remember that there have also been a number of severe tragedies along the way. But despite several serious setbacks, the cavern has survived! It is still very much alive and well. So the ending has truly not yet been written. To quote Mark "Chogon" DeForest of Cyan: "It's not over until the last Bahro sings."

The D'ni's culture is very rich and vast, and D'ni history continues to unfold before our very eyes. We explorers still have so much to learn about them, something that could last a whole lifetime.

Lastly, a big thank you goes to Jeff Zandi, who shared much with me about past events and his insights. Thanks to the DRC, who did (and probably continue to do) all they can to see D'ni restored to it's former glory. To Cyan, who introduced us to the D'ni universe thought their work and our computers. I'd also like to thank my friends at the Guild of Messengers for giving me support and guidance when writing this series. And to DimensionTravelerCalum of Heritage Night for sharing his archives.

...but above all, a big thank you to all of the numerous explorers and friends who gave me the inspiration to write all this!


<PB><PB><font style=n size=12><p align=center>Appendix 1


<font style=b size=9><p align=center>MYST & URU related computer games

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>Myst. September 1993. A single-player (SP) puzzle & adventure game. Cyan.

Riven. October 1997. SP puzzle & adventure game. Cyan.

Myst III: Exile. May 2001. SP puzzle & adventure game. Presto Studios.

Uru: Ages Beyond Myst (ABM). November 2003. SP puzzle & adventure game. Cyan Worlds.

Uru Live (also called Prologue). Massively Multiplayer Online game (MMO), from November 2003 to February 2004. Cyan Worlds. More of a test project, widening the Myst universe and mythology, but it was in the end aborted. Composed of Choru, Ubiru and Rehearsal.

Uru: To D'ni. March 2004. Expansion pack to ABM. SP puzzle & adventure game. Cyan Worlds (also released as URU: Complete Chronicles)

Uru: The Path of the Shell (TPotS). July 2004. Second expansion pack to ABM. SP puzzle & adventure game. Cyan Worlds.

Uru: Complete Chronicles (CC). Bundles Uru and its two expansion packs together. Cyan Worlds.

Untìl Uru. August 2004. Online playable, modified version of ABM. Cyan Worlds.

Myst IV: Revelations. Sept. 2004. SP puzzle & adventure game. Ubisoft.

Myst V: End of Ages. Sept. 2005. SP puzzle & adventure game. Cyan Worlds.

D'mala Shard. MMO from March 2006 to February 2007. Cyan Worlds. Cyan's Untìl Uru shard.

Myst Online: Uru Live (MO:UL). MMO from the end of February 2007 to April 2008. Cyan Worlds & GameTap.

Myst Online: Uru Live (again) (MO:ULa). MMO from February 2010 and ongoing. Cyan Worlds.


<PB><font style=n size=12><p align=center>Appendix 2


<font style=b size=9><p align=center>DRC Contractors

<font style=n size=10><p align=left>Cunningham, Michelle: Translator for the DRC until 2004.

Harrington, Matthew: Head of the DRC's translator team until 2004.

Henderson, Phil: Restoration Engineer for the DRC. Believed to have been killed in late 2003 but appeared anew in the City in 2007, stating to have been in Noloben and other Ages along with the Bahro.

Lawson, Tricia: Translator for the DRC.

Michaels, Sonya: Journalist for a Maine newspaper, an acquaintance of Douglas Sharper and a part-time worker for the DRC.

Reynolds, Sam: Translator for the DRC until 2004. Has worked on the documentation regarding Teledahn and more extensively on the D'ni Kings.

Sharper, Douglas: An adventurer and hunter by trade. Had control of and oversaw the documentation of Teledahn. Last one to leave the Cavern in 2004. Returned in 2007. Sharper along with Nick White went on several expeditions to the Pod Ages and to Noloben to study the Bahro.

Simpson, Michael: Made reports on Gahreesen and Watcher's Sanctuary.
Left the Cavern in 2004.

White, Nick: Restoration Engineer for the DRC and translator, in association with Sharper. Was said to have "hidden" some translations from the DRC and was later suspended by Cate Alexander for "fraternization".]]></translation>
            </element>
        </set>
    </age>
</localizations>
